When synthesized with the 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (bdc) linker, MIL-125 has an optical band gap in the UV region (ca. 3.6 $\mathrm{eV}/345\mathrm{nm}$ ; see Figure 3a) and is an active photocatalyst for the oxidation of alcohols to aldehydes.2 $\mathsf{\Omega}^{3}\mathrm{~MIL}\cdot125\mathrm{-N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ made with the monoaminated bdc- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ linker25 was reported to be a photocatalyst with visible-light-induced activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction26 and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production.27 The bdc- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ linker was shown to be responsible for the extra absorption band in the visible region, indicative of a band gap reduction (to ca. $2.6~\mathrm{eV}/\$ $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ ).25−28 Garci ́a and co-workers further confirmed the ability of $\mathbf{MIL-}125{\cdot}\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ to undergo photoinduced charge separation.29 A similar band gap reduction and red shift were reported for ${\mathrm{Zr–UiO}}–66$ upon synthesis with bdc- $\cdot\mathbf{NH}_{2}.$ , emphasizing the electronic role of the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ group in determining the optical response in MOFs.30 Coincidently, aminated linkers are becoming particularly popular in porous MOF synthesis, as polar $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups are expected to enhance CO2 capture, yield selective gas adsorption,31−35 and enable postsynthetic modifications.36−38
These recent findings make it necessary to develop strategies to control the band gap of MIL-125. Here we performed a combined experimental and computational study to elucidate the band structure of MIL-125 and the impact of functional groups on the bdc linker, exploring the observed red shift in MIL-125- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ . We then computationally predicted the beneficial impact of using the diaminated bdc- $(\mathbf{NH}_{2})_{2}$ linker, which was confirmed by a targeted synthesis. We finally explored in silico other potential bdc linkers (Figure 2) as candidates for optical control in MIL-125.
Amines are ring-activating substituents; as an initial study, we investigated the change in the band gap in response to increasing the concentration of monoaminated bdc- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ . MIL-125 (Figure 3a) and three aminated MIL-125 analogues (Figure $3\mathrm{b-d}$ ) were synthesized under solvothermal conditions by varying the ratio of bdc and bdc- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ linkers (i.e., $10\%,$ , $50\%$ , and $100\%$ bdc- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{NH}_{2},$ equating to ${\sim}1$ , 6, and 12 bdc- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ linkers per unit cell). Figure 3 presents scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of these four samples and their accompanying UV spectra and powder colors. It is apparent that the absorption onset (ca. $475~\mathrm{nm},$ is similar for all of the monoaminated samples. The samples also demonstrated similar band gaps of ${\sim}2.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ [Table S2 in the Supporting Information (SI)], but the molar extinction coefficient notably increased in proportion with the bdc- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ content. This feature was reflected in the physical crystal appearance: the yellow color increased in intensity. This characteristic absorption pattern suggests that a single $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ motif is responsible for the reduced band gap of the monoaminated series of MIL-125 analogues.
Electronic structure calculations on the same series of monoaminated $\mathbf{MIL}–125–\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ solids were performed using density functional theory (DFT) as described in the SI. Figure 4a,b depicts the valence band (VB) and conduction band (CB) orbitals, respectively, for standard MIL-125. The upper VB is composed of bdc aromatic $\mathsf{2p}$ orbitals. Introducing a single bdc$\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ linker per unit cell (i.e., to give $10\%\mathrm{-MIL-}12\bar{5}–\mathbf{NH}_{2}\bar{\ y}$ breaks the original I4/mmm symmetry and splits the VB into a highenergy occupied state (depicted in blue in Figure $\mathrm{4c}^{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ ). The other aromatic bands are also split within $0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ of each other and contribute to the VB-1,2...12. Because of the strong electrondonating characteristics of aromatic amines, the modified VB is $1.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ above that of MIL-125 (depicted with the blue arrow in Figure $\mathsf{4c}$ ), resulting in the lower band gap. This localized electronic modification results in a flat band in k space, which emphasizes the absence of long-range chemical interactions.
Further calculations were carried out by systematically increasing the amine content from 1 to 12 bdc- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ linkers per unit cell (Figure 4d). It is apparent that the VB is modified upon amination of the linker while the lower CB is unchanged. Thus, a single $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group electronically saturates MIL-125. The band structure of $10\%\mathrm{-MIL\mathrm{-}}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (blue in Figure 4c) depicts a localized noninteracting state, which was observed for all of the monoaminated systems independent of the bdc- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ content.
The amine functional motif plays a crucial role in the band structure of MIL-125. We therefore considered the plausible extension of further increasing the electron density of the aromatic motifs through polyamination of the bdc linker. Selecting diaminated bdc- $(\dot{\bf N}\dot{\bf H}_{2})_{2}$ (Figure 2 right) as a potentially interesting linker, we constructed a $10\%$ -MIL-125- $\bar{{\bf\Phi}}({\bf N H}_{2})_{2}$ model as a mixture of $10\%$ bdc- $(\mathbf{NH}_{2})_{2}$ and $90\%$ bdc and computed its band structure (shown in green in Figure $\mathrm{4c}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ). This model may be compared to its monoaminated analogue (shown in blue in Figure $_{4c}$ ). Our calculations predicted that $10\%$ -MIL125- $(\mathbf{NH}_{2})_{2}$ would exhibit a larger red shift due to the increase in aromatic electronic density, with a resultant band gap of $1.28\:\mathrm{eV}$ .
This presents a further band gap decrease of $1.1\mathrm{eV}$ relative to the $10\%$ -monoaminated analogue.
We subsequently targeted the synthesis of a diaminated MIL125 derivative, which was successfully achieved with a mixture of $10\%$ bdc- $\left(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\right)_{2}$ and $90\%$ bdc- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ (Figure 3e). This compound differs from the $10\%$ -MIL-125- $\left(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\right)_{2}$ model described in the previous paragraph by the presence of bdc$\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ instead of bdc. We did not isolate the $10\%$ -MIL-125- $(\mathbf{NH}_{2})_{2},$ but our compound should act electronically in a similar fashion, since the amine motifs are indeed noninteracting and produce highly localized electronic bands. Optical measurements (Figure 3e) revealed a further band gap decrease of $1.1\mathrm{eV}$ for this solid relative to the monoaminated samples to give an optical band gap of $1.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ , shifting the absorption onset to the red/IR region. This is in striking agreement with the above computational prediction. To approximate the blended solid, we further computed the electronic structure of a mixed $10\%$ -MIL-125- $(\mathbf{NH_{2}})_{2}/90\%\mathbf{-MIL-1}25\mathbf{-NH_{2}}$ model (shown in Figure 4c in orange). In both the experimental spectrum and the band structure calculations we observed the presence of the monoamine motifs. The multiple orange bands in the $1{-}1.5\mathrm{eV}$ region are the result of the inequivalent monoamines. Our computational approach predicted a band gap of $1.55\ \mathrm{eV}$ (orange in Figure 5). The difference between the experimental $(1.3\mathrm{eV})$ and computed $(1.55\ \mathrm{eV})$ band gaps is attributed to the challenge of describing this partially disordered system computationally, including using lattice constants fixed at the equilibrium values of the parent MIL-125 structure.
Finally, we extended the computational exploration to three potential MIL-125 analogues. Amines are one of the stronger electron-donating substituents; weaker electron-donating substituents should reduce the band gap to a lesser extent.39,40 The introduction of $10\%$ bdc-R $\mathrm{{'R}=-O H},$ $-\mathrm{CH}_{3},$ $-\mathrm{Cl};$ Figure 2 left) per unit cell resulted in flexible band gap control between 3.5 and $2.4~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 5). The weakest electron-donating group, ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3},$ moderately decreases the band gap to $3.5~\mathrm{eV}.$ Aromatic halides are more chemically complex and may be either weakly electron-donating or -withdrawing. In MIL-125, $-\mathrm{Cl}$ acts as a weak electron donor with a predicted band gap similar to that for the ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ group. The $-\mathrm{OH}$ group is more electron-donating than ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ and $-\mathrm{Cl},$ which is reflected by the predicted band gap of $2.8~\mathrm{eV}$ for the $10\%$ -MIL-125-OH model. The $-\mathbf{Br}$ and ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ substituents were also computed. Our results suggest that $-\mathrm{Br}$ motifs produce band gap control similar to $-\mathrm{Cl},$ but the crystal could not be stabilized above eight substitutions per unit cell. The ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ group is strongly electron-withdrawing, potentially lowering a $\mathbf{CB}{+n}$ band into the band gap. It was found that the inclusion of ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ resulted in destabilization of the Ti−O bond, and a single substitution produced an unstable crystal. Thus, $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ , $-\mathrm{OH},$ , $\mathrm{-CH}_{3},$ and $-\mathrm{Cl}$ are the most promising and favorable substitutions.
In conclusion, we have elucidated the specific role of the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ group of the monoaminated bdc- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ linker in lowering the optical band gap of the titanium-containing MIL- $\cdot125\ –\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ (ca. $2.6~\mathrm{eV},$ ). We confirmed that electronic modifications of the aromatic motifs are localized and directly control the optical properties through modification of the valence band. DFT calculations showed that the optical response of MIL-125 may be tailored toward absorption in the visible region through rational selection of substituents of the aromatic bdc linker. The diaminated bdc- $(\mathbf{NH}_{2})_{2}$ linker was expected to demonstrate the most significant red shift. This prediction was confirmed by the synthesis of $10\%$ -MIL-1 $25{\mathrm{-}}\big(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\big)_{2}/90\%{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{MIL}{\mathrm{-}}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ (1.3 $\mathrm{eV}/950~\mathrm{nm})$ . From the experimental perspective, we have described the synthesis of a MIL-125 derivative with mixed aminated linkers. This methodology can be further extended to the large range of aromatic linkers used in MOF synthesis, which should enable accurate and predictable band gap control in tailored frameworks. For example, the $Z\mathrm{n}$ −bdc-based IRMOF$1^{14,41}$ has an electronic structure comparable to that of MIL-125 $(3.57\mathrm{eV}),^{39}$ thus posing potential for similar control of its band gap through the modifications proposed here.
